<%- include("../../partials/portal-head", { title: "Request received" }) %>

<section class="section-tight">
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Student portal</p>
    <% var dup = typeof duplicate !== "undefined" && duplicate; %>

    <% if (dup) { %>
      <h1>You have already sent a request</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        <% if (dup === "approved") { %>
          This address has already been approved. Check your email for the
          link to set your password, including the spam folder.
        <% } else { %>
          We already have a request from this address and it is waiting to
          be looked at. Please wait for the approval email.
        <% } %>
      </p>
    <% } else { %>
      <h1>Thank you — we have your request</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Someone from the office will look at it and reply by email, usually
        within a working day.
      </p>
    <% } %>

    <%#
      A submission the spam check refused lands here too, wording and
      all, so a bot is never told it was caught. A repeat request is
      now named explicitly above: staying silent about it made the form
      look broken, which was worse than admitting an address has asked.
    %>
    <div class="notice" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <div>
        <strong>What happens next</strong>
        <ul style="margin:.5rem 0 0 1.1rem;padding:0">
          <li>We read what you wrote and decide.</li>
          <li>If it is approved, you get an email with a link to choose your password.</li>
          <li>If not, we write and tell you why, and how to reach us.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="muted" style="font-size:.88rem;margin-top:1.5rem">
      Nothing arrived after a day or two? Check your spam folder, then
      <a href="/contact">contact the office</a> — we would rather hear twice than not at all.
    </p>

    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/" style="margin-top:1rem">Back to the site</a>
  </div>
</section>

<%- include("../../partials/portal-foot") %>
